---
import LessonLayout from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import ClaimLabel from '../../components/ClaimLabel.astro';
import Cite from '../../components/learn/Cite.astro';
import { INSTRUMENTS, QUESTIONS, REVIEWED, REVIEWED_LABEL, SOURCES, sourceNumber } from '../../lib/learn/money';
import { href } from '../../lib/url';
import '../../styles/learn.css';

// The comparison shown before any choice (and without JavaScript).
const DEFAULT_COMPARE = ['cash', 'deposits', 'stablecoins'];
const compared = INSTRUMENTS.filter((i) => DEFAULT_COMPARE.includes(i.id));
const n = sourceNumber;
---
<LessonLayout
  title="What Is Money?"
  description="Cash, bank deposits, central bank reserves, Treasury bills, money market funds, payment stablecoins, bitcoin and gold, compared: whose promise each is, its legal status, who can hold it, how payments settle and what backs it."
  lead="Cash, a bank balance, a Treasury bill, a stablecoin and a gold coin can all be called money. They are very different things. This lesson compares eight of them: whose promise each one is, who can hold it, how a payment with it settles, and what stands behind it."
  reviewed={REVIEWED}
  minutes={12}
>
  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="functions">
    <h2 id="functions">What people mean by money</h2>
    <p class="claim-line"><ClaimLabel kind="model" /> <span class="small muted">The standard textbook description</span></p>
    <p>
      Economics textbooks describe money by three jobs it does. In the words of the Federal
      Reserve's education program:<Cite ids={['fed-education-functions']} number={n} />
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Medium of exchange:</strong> "money is widely accepted as a method of payment."</li>
      <li><strong>Unit of account:</strong> money works as "a yardstick," used "to measure value in economic transactions."</li>
      <li><strong>Store of value:</strong> money you earn today "will hold its value until tomorrow, next week, or even next year."</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Not everything that does one of these jobs does all three, and they come in degrees: a
      currency that loses value quickly is a poor store of value but can still be a good means
      of payment.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Commodity money</strong> "had value because it was made of a substance that had
      value," like gold or silver coins. <strong>Fiat money</strong> "does not have intrinsic
      value and does not represent an asset in a vault somewhere"; its value comes from being
      declared legal tender by the government that issues it.<Cite ids={['fed-education-functions']} number={n} />
      The US dollar is fiat money: Federal Reserve notes "are not redeemable in gold, silver,
      or any other commodity."<Cite ids={['fed-currency-faq']} number={n} />
    </p>
    <p>
      The Federal Reserve's own measure of the money supply is narrower than everything this
      lesson compares: "cash, coins, and balances in bank accounts."<Cite ids={['fed-money-supply']} number={n} />
      Its broader measure, M2, adds small time deposits and retail money market fund shares.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="promise">
    <h2 id="promise">Money as a promise</h2>
    <p class="claim-line"><ClaimLabel kind="fact" /> <span class="small muted">Whose liability each one is</span></p>
    <p>
      Much of what people call money is somebody's debt. The cash in your wallet is an
      obligation of the United States and a liability of the Federal Reserve; the balance in
      your bank account is a debt your bank owes you. As the Federal Reserve puts it, the money
      people use in everyday digital payments is made of "liabilities of private entities, such
      as commercial banks."<Cite ids={['usc-12-411', 'fed-balance-sheet', 'fed-money-payments']} number={n} />
      Every asset on this list is held by someone; the question is who, if anyone, owes it to
      them.
    </p>
  </section>
  <ul class="promise-list" aria-label="Who owes the holder of each kind of money">
    {INSTRUMENTS.map((i) => (
      <li class:list={{ 'is-none': i.noIssuer }}>
        <strong>{i.name}</strong>
        <span class="promise-list__arrow" aria-hidden="true">owed by</span>
        <span class="promise-list__who"><span class="vh">is owed by </span>{i.owedBy}</span>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <div class="prose lesson-section">
    <p>
      Five of the eight are a debt with a fixed amount in dollars. A money market fund share is
      different: it is a share of what a fund owns, not a debt. Bitcoin and gold are no one's
      debt at all: their value is whatever buyers will pay.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="money-compare" aria-labelledby="compare-heading" data-money-compare data-mode="side">
    <h2 id="compare-heading">Compare them</h2>
    <p>
      Choose up to three to see side by side, or pick one question to see every answer to it.
      Numbers in brackets link to the sources at the end of the lesson.
    </p>
    <div class="money-compare__controls" data-js-only>
      <fieldset class="segmented">
        <legend>View</legend>
        <div class="segmented__options">
          <label><input type="radio" name="mc-mode" value="side" checked /><span>Side by side</span></label>
          <label><input type="radio" name="mc-mode" value="question" /><span>One question</span></label>
        </div>
      </fieldset>
      <fieldset data-for="side">
        <legend>Kinds of money <span class="small muted" data-out="compare-hint">Choose up to 3.</span></legend>
        <div class="pick-group">
          {INSTRUMENTS.map((i) => (
            <label class="pick"><input type="checkbox" name="mc-item" value={i.id} checked={DEFAULT_COMPARE.includes(i.id)} /><span>{i.name}</span></label>
          ))}
        </div>
      </fieldset>
      <fieldset data-for="question">
        <legend>Question</legend>
        <div class="pick-group">
          {QUESTIONS.map((q, k) => (
            <label class="pick"><input type="radio" name="mc-question" value={q.key} checked={k === 0} /><span>{q.label}</span></label>
          ))}
        </div>
      </fieldset>
    </div>
    <div data-out="compare">
      <div class="table-wrap" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label={`Comparing ${compared.map((i) => i.name.toLowerCase()).join(', ')}`}>
        <table class="money-table">
          <caption>Comparing {compared.map((i) => i.name.toLowerCase()).join(', ')}</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th>{compared.map((i) => <th scope="col">{i.name}</th>)}</tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {QUESTIONS.map((q) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{q.label}</th>
                {compared.map((i) => <td>{i.answers[q.key].text}<Cite ids={i.answers[q.key].sources} number={n} /></td>)}
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="vh" data-out="compare-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
    <p class="small muted calc__nojs">The full comparison of all eight is below. Choosing which to compare needs JavaScript.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="all-eight">
    <h2 id="all-eight">All eight in detail</h2>
    <ul class="instrument-grid">
      {INSTRUMENTS.map((i) => (
        <li class="instrument" id={`money-${i.id}`}>
          <h3>{i.name}</h3>
          <p class="instrument__summary">{i.summary}</p>
          <dl>
            {QUESTIONS.map((q) => (
              <div>
                <dt>{q.label}</dt>
                <dd>{i.answers[q.key].text}<Cite ids={i.answers[q.key].sources} number={n} /></dd>
              </div>
            ))}
          </dl>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="why">
    <h2 id="why">Why the differences matter</h2>
    <p class="claim-line"><ClaimLabel kind="interpretation" /> <span class="small muted">What follows from the facts above</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Whose promise you rely on.</strong> A promise is only as good as whoever makes it.
        Cash and reserves rest on the Federal Reserve and the US government; a deposit on your
        bank, with insurance up to $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank, per ownership
        category; a payment stablecoin on its issuer and its reserves; a Treasury bill on the
        United States.<Cite ids={['fdic-faq', 'genius-act', 'treasury-marketable']} number={n} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Insurance is narrower than it looks.</strong> FDIC insurance covers deposits at
        insured banks. It does not cover money market funds, Treasury securities or crypto
        assets, "even if they were purchased from an insured bank," and the GENIUS Act rules it
        out for payment stablecoins.<Cite ids={['fdic-not-insured', 'fdic-crypto', 'genius-act']} number={n} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Where a payment finally settles.</strong> When a payment passes between
        customers of two different banks, the banks settle with each other by moving reserves
        between their accounts at the Federal Reserve, for example over Fedwire, where a
        payment is final once processed. That is why reserves matter even though the public
        never holds them.<Cite ids={['fed-fedwire', 'fed-iorb-faq']} number={n} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A fixed dollar amount, or a market price.</strong> Cash, deposits and reserves
        are always worth their face value in dollars. A Treasury bill pays its face value at
        maturity; a stablecoin is meant to hold its value. Bitcoin and gold have no face value
        in dollars at all: their price is set by the market, and virtual currencies "are more
        volatile than traditional fiat currencies."<Cite ids={['treasury-bills', 'genius-act', 'cftc-virtual-currency']} number={n} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not interchangeable.</strong> Each of these can serve as money for some purposes
        and some people. None does every job equally well, and switching from one to another
        changes whose promise you hold.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="key-ideas" aria-labelledby="key-ideas">
    <h2 id="key-ideas">Key ideas</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Money is described by three jobs: a means of payment, a unit of account and a store of value.</li>
      <li>Most money is a promise: cash and reserves are liabilities of the Federal Reserve, deposits of your bank, stablecoins of their issuer.</li>
      <li>Only US coins and currency are legal tender; bank deposits, reserves, Treasury bills, fund shares, stablecoins and bitcoin are not.</li>
      <li>FDIC insurance covers bank deposits up to the standard amount, and nothing else on this list.</li>
      <li>Banks can settle payments with each other by moving reserves at the Federal Reserve, where a Fedwire payment is final.</li>
      <li>Bitcoin and gold are no one's liability; their dollar price floats.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="related">
    <h2 id="related">Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={href('calculators/apr-apy/')}>APR and APY calculator</a>: what a savings rate really pays.</li>
      <li><a href={href('calculators/savings-growth/')}>High-yield savings calculator</a>: how a bank deposit grows.</li>
      <li><a href={href('learn/how-banks-create-money/')}>How Banks Create Money</a>: what happens on balance sheets when a bank lends.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose lesson-section" aria-labelledby="sources">
    <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
    <p class="small">Every source was checked on {REVIEWED_LABEL}. Facts that can change (the interest rate on reserves, the status of stablecoin rules) are stated as of that date.</p>
    <ol class="source-list small">
      {SOURCES.map((s) => (
        <li id={`src-${s.id}`}>{s.publisher}, <a href={s.url}>{s.title}</a>, {s.date}.</li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>
</LessonLayout>

<script>
  import '../../scripts/learn/money';
</script>
